findings.problem:
The paper overstates its compliance with the AGMAI guidance. The AGMAI 29 Sep guidelines ('Responsible Release of AI-Generated Mathematics', pathway 2B) list: model name, prompts, a summarized chain of thought, time taken, estimated compute cost, and per-problem documentation. The paper gives:
- only the model family, with the version available on request;
- only the subagent prompts (dev/workflows), not the author's instructions to the orchestrating session;
- no reasoning summaries, no wall-clock times and no cost;
- no token use for the orchestrating session, which wrote the Lean statement, PROBLEM.md, PLAN.md and the paper.
The guidance is also mainly addressed to AI labs. The citation is wrong: it points to the AGMAI homepage, not the guidelines, and the howpublished field is garbled (it prints as 'and 26 Oct 2026, 2026').

'Carefully reviewed' implies more than AI-only review, which is all that took place. 'Pre-registered' and 'fixed' have no verifiable anchor:
- the paper gives no commit hash, file hashes or timestamp;
- the repository has no commits yet;
- PLAN A0 itself requires a 'timestamped hash';
- the plan expects the targets to change: 'frozen after the skeleton exists (week 4)', with spec decisions pending 'before the v1.0 freeze';
- an earlier timestamped candidate statement exists (Remondiere2026).

There is no competing-interests or funding declaration, which COPE and ICMJE expect. A reader would want one here:
- all the work was done with Anthropic's products;
- the paper makes evaluative statements about a competitor's (OpenAI's) process and claims;
- the library records that Anthropic posted a rival ETH result;
- one of the cited Sussman–Alpöge authors is an Anthropic employee.

The disclosure omits facts that are readily available and that matter for calibration (they are AGMAI's 'time taken' item):
- wall-clock times and tool calls for three of the four workflows;
- that the orchestrating session, not any subagent, wrote the Lean statement (v0.1 and the v0.2 fixes), PROBLEM.md and PLAN.md, and that its usage is excluded;
- that all subagents used one model;
- that the entire project took place on 5–6 November 2026.
